1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The PEC is a dual-channel digital controller for each propeller.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
It manages the propeller speed and the blade angle (pitch) in all flight phases.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
For each engine, we have one PEC unit with two independent channels, Channel A and Channel B.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Usually, one channel is active while the other one is in standby.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
If the active channel fails, the standby channel takes over automatically without any crew action.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The PEC receives inputs from the Power Management selector, the Power Lever, and the Condition Lever.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
By processing these inputs, it maintains the target Np (propeller speed) for the selected configuration.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It also provides the synchrophasing function to keep the noise level in the cabin as low as possible.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Power supply for the PEC comes from the Permanent Magnet Alternator (PMA) of its respective engine.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
This means the PEC remains functional even if there's a total loss of the aircraft's electrical DC system.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
On the overhead panel, we have the PEC pushbuttons with FAULT and OFF indicators.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The FAULT light comes on if the PEC detects a major internal failure or a signal disagreement.

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
If both channels are lost, the PEC is automatically set to OFF and the propeller is in backup mode.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
In this backup mode, the propeller speed is controlled by the mechanical governor, but the Np might fluctuate.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Regarding the PEC test, it is a ground procedure to check the safety functions of the system.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
During the test, we verify that the feathering logic and overspeed protection work correctly.

17
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The test is inhibited in flight to prevent any unintended propeller behavior while in the air.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
When you perform the test, you will see the Np drop slightly as the PEC tests its ability to feather.

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
If the test fails, a maintenance action is required before the next flight.

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The PEC is also linked with the EEC for engine power coordination and auto-feathering logic.

21
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Overall, the PEC simplifies the pilot's workload by managing the propeller automatically.
